  <!-- Section 5: Designing Lounge Sets -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Designing Lounge Sets | Creating Coordinated Comfort Looks</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Lounge sets - coordinating tops and bottoms - are loungewear's most popular category. Designing successful lounge sets requires balancing comfort, style, and practicality while ensuring both pieces work together aesthetically. Following design principles helps create lounge sets that resonate with customers.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Color and Pattern Coordination</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Successful lounge sets feature coordinated colors or patterns. Solid color sets in neutral tones (gray, navy, black, beige) offer versatility and broad appeal. Matching colors create cohesive looks. Patterned sets - stripes, florals, geometric prints - add visual interest and fashion appeal. When using patterns, ensure both pieces use the same pattern or complementary colors. Color blocking - different but coordinating colors on top and bottom - creates dynamic looks. Consider your target market's color preferences when designing sets. Our <a href="/tech-pack-service">tech pack service</a> helps develop coordinated color specifications.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Fabric Consistency</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Use the same fabric for both top and bottom pieces to ensure consistency in feel, drape, and care requirements. Different fabrics may wash differently or fade at different rates, creating mismatched appearances over time. If using different fabrics (e.g., fleece top with jersey bottom), ensure colors coordinate and care instructions are compatible. Fabric weight should be consistent - heavy tops with light bottoms feel unbalanced. Our <a href="/fabric-sourcing">fabric sourcing</a> ensures fabric consistency across set pieces.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Top Design Considerations</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Lounge set tops include hoodies, sweatshirts, relaxed tees, or casual button-front shirts. Prioritize comfort with relaxed fits, soft fabrics, and gentle necklines. Features like kangaroo pockets (hoodies), ribbed cuffs and hems, and quality construction add value. Consider sleeve length - long sleeves for winter, short sleeves for summer. Neckline options include crewneck, V-neck, or hooded. Ensure top proportions balance with bottom style. Oversized tops pair with fitted bottoms, while fitted tops work with relaxed bottoms.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Bottom Design Considerations</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Lounge set bottoms include joggers, lounge pants, or shorts. Essential features include elastic waistbands with drawstrings for adjustability, comfortable inseams appropriate for the style, and practical pockets. Joggers feature cuffed ankles for a polished look. Lounge pants may have straight or tapered legs. Shorts should have appropriate inseam length - too short feels inappropriate, too long defeats the purpose. Ensure bottom style coordinates with top - joggers pair with hoodies, lounge pants with relaxed tees.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Practical Features</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Include practical features that enhance comfort and functionality. Pockets on bottoms and some tops add utility. Drawstring waists allow fit adjustment. Ribbed cuffs and hems maintain shape and add polish. Quality stitching and durable construction ensure longevity. Consider washing requirements - loungewear should be machine washable for convenience. Avoid delicate trims or complicated care instructions that deter customers. Practical features enhance perceived value and customer satisfaction.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 6: FAQ -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
